%% ============================================================
%% 13.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:lang-design-intro}

The design of a programming language is an exercise in applied philosophy,
mathematics, and engineering simultaneously.  Every decision---whether to
expose memory management to the programmer, whether types should be checked
statically or dynamically, whether functions are first-class values---carries
consequences that ripple through the entire ecosystem: the compiler, the
runtime, the libraries, the tools, and ultimately the humans who must read and
write code in the language.

This chapter examines the fundamental principles that guide language design.
We do not pretend that these principles form a consistent, complete theory;
they often conflict, and the art of language design lies in navigating those
conflicts with taste and judgment.  Our goal is to make the trade-offs
explicit, to illustrate them with concrete examples from influential languages,
and to provide a framework for evaluating design decisions.

\begin{insight}
A programming language is a theory, and a compiler or interpreter is a
model of that theory.  The better the theory, the more useful the model.
Language design is therefore the art of constructing theories of computation
that are simultaneously expressive, analyzable, and usable.
\end{insight}

We organize the discussion around six central tensions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Expressiveness versus analyzability} (\Cref{sec:expressiveness-vs-analyzability}).
  \item \textbf{Static versus dynamic checking} (\Cref{sec:static-vs-dynamic}).
  \item \textbf{Orthogonality and minimalism} (\Cref{sec:orthogonality-minimalism}).
  \item \textbf{Abstraction mechanisms} (\Cref{sec:abstraction-mechanisms}).
  \item \textbf{Safety guarantees} (\Cref{sec:safety-guarantees}).
  \item \textbf{Case studies} in influential languages (\Cref{sec:lang-design-case-studies}).
\end{enumerate}

%% ============================================================
%% 13.2 Expressiveness vs. Analyzability
%% ============================================================
\section{Expressiveness versus Analyzability}\label{sec:expressiveness-vs-analyzability}

A programming language must be \emph{expressive} enough to encode the
algorithms and data structures that programmers need, yet \emph{analyzable}
enough that compilers and verification tools can reason about programs
effectively.  These two goals are in constant tension.

\subsection{The Expressiveness Hierarchy}\label{sec:expressiveness-hierarchy}

We can organize languages by their \emph{computational expressiveness}---the
class of functions they can compute.  By the Church--Turing thesis, any
Turing-complete language can compute the same class of partial recursive
functions.  But expressiveness is not merely about computability; it also
concerns \emph{how naturally} a computation can be expressed.

\begin{definition}[Expressive Power]\label{def:expressive-power}
  Language $L_1$ is \emph{more expressive} than language $L_2$ (written
  $L_2 \sqsubset L_1$) if there exists a program $p \in L_1$ such that no
  program $q \in L_2$ computes the same input--output relation, \emph{or} if
  for every computable relation there is a program in $L_1$ whose size is
  asymptotically smaller than any program in $L_2$ computing the same
  relation.
\end{definition}

The first clause captures absolute expressiveness (what can be computed);
the second captures \emph{succinctness} (how concisely it can be expressed).
Both matter in practice.

\begin{example}\label{ex:expressiveness-examples}
  Consider the following expressiveness comparisons:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Regular languages vs.\ context-free languages}: A regular
      expression can match patterns, but a context-free grammar can match
      balanced parentheses---something no regular expression can do.
    \item \textbf{Total vs.\ partial functions}: A language that guarantees
      termination (e.g., Dhall, Charity) cannot express all Turing-computable
      functions, but every program can be analyzed for termination.
    \item \textbf{First-order vs.\ higher-order}: A first-order language
      cannot pass functions as arguments, limiting its ability to express
      generic algorithms like \texttt{map} and \texttt{fold}.
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{The Analyzability Spectrum}\label{sec:analyzability-spectrum}

\emph{Analyzability} refers to the extent to which properties of programs can
be determined by static analysis.  At one extreme, a language like
\textbf{BitC} or \textbf{SPARK/Ada} allows nearly complete static
verification; at the other, a language like \textbf{Perl} or \textbf{Bourne
Shell} makes even basic properties undecidable.

\begin{definition}[Static Analyzability]\label{def:analyzability}
  A language $L$ is \emph{statically analyzable} for property $P$ if there
  exists a decidable procedure that, given any program $p \in L$, determines
  whether $p$ satisfies $P$.
\end{definition}

By Rice's theorem, any non-trivial semantic property is undecidable for
Turing-complete languages.  Therefore, language designers face a fundamental
choice:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Restrict expressiveness} to make more properties decidable
    (e.g., total functional languages).
  \item \textbf{Accept undecidability} but provide useful approximations
    (e.g., type systems, abstract interpretation).
  \item \textbf{Defer checking to runtime} (e.g., dynamic typing, contracts).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Trade-off in Practice}\label{sec:expressiveness-tradeoff}

The trade-off between expressiveness and analyzability manifests in many
specific design decisions:

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Expressiveness vs.\ Analyzability Trade-offs}
  \label{tab:expr-vs-analyze}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \hline
    \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Expressiveness} & \textbf{Analyzability} \\
    \hline
    General recursion & $+$ & $-$ \\
    First-class continuations & $+$ & $-$ \\
    Dependent types & $+$ & $-$ (in general) \\
    Dynamic typing & $+$ & $-$ \\
    Eval / reflection & $+$ & $-$ \\
    Total functions only & $-$ & $+$ \\
    Effect systems & $-$ & $+$ \\
    Linear types & $-$ & $+$ \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{theorem}[Expressiveness--Analyzability Tension]\label{thm:expr-analyze-tension}
  For any Turing-complete language $L$, there exist non-trivial semantic
  properties that are undecidable.  Therefore, increasing the
  expressiveness of $L$ (while maintaining Turing completeness) necessarily
  increases the set of undecidable properties.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  This is a direct consequence of Rice's theorem~\cite{rice1953unsolvable}.
  Let $\mathcal{P}$ be any non-trivial property of partial recursive
  functions.  If $L$ is Turing-complete, then the set of programs in $L$
  computing functions with property $\mathcal{P}$ is undecidable.  Adding
  expressive features to $L$ (while preserving Turing completeness) can only
  increase the class of representable partial functions, and thus the set of
  undecidable properties.
\end{proof}

%% ============================================================
%% 13.3 Static vs. Dynamic Checking
%% ============================================================
\section{Static versus Dynamic Checking}\label{sec:static-vs-dynamic}

The question of \emph{when} to check properties---at compile time or at run
time---is one of the most consequential decisions in language design.  It
affects performance, reliability, development speed, and the entire tooling
ecosystem.

\subsection{The Static Checking Philosophy}\label{sec:static-philosophy}

Static checking asserts that as many errors as possible should be detected
before the program runs.  The benefits are clear:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Early error detection}: Errors are caught during development,
    not in production.
  \item \textbf{Documentation}: Types serve as machine-checked documentation.
  \item \textbf{Optimization}: Static information enables aggressive
    compilation (unboxing, inlining, specialization).
  \item \textbf{Refactoring}: Type information makes large-scale code
    transformation safe.
  \item \textbf{Tooling}: IDEs, linters, and verifiers all benefit from
    static information.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Static Type Soundness]\label{def:static-soundness}
  A type system is \emph{sound} if well-typed programs do not go wrong.
  Formally, if $\vdash p : \tau$ then the execution of $p$ either terminates
  with a value of type $\tau$ or diverges, but never produces a
  \emph{runtime type error}.
\end{definition}

The classical formulation of type soundness uses two properties:

\begin{theorem}[Progress and Preservation]\label{thm:progress-preservation}
  A type system is sound if it satisfies:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Progress}: If $\vdash e : \tau$ then either $e$ is a value
      or there exists $e'$ such that $e \stepsto e'$.
    \item \textbf{Preservation}: If $\vdash e : \tau$ and $e \stepsto e'$
      then $\vdash e' : \tau$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

This theorem, due to Wright and Felleasen~\cite{wright1994syntactic}, has
become the standard way to prove type soundness.  We explore it in depth in
\Cref{ch:small-step}.

\subsection{The Dynamic Checking Philosophy}\label{sec:dynamic-philosophy}

Dynamic checking defers error detection to runtime.  Its advocates argue:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Flexibility}: Programs can manipulate data of unknown or
    varying types (prototyping, scripting, DSLs).
  \item \textbf{Rapid development}: No time spent satisfying a type checker;
    iterate faster.
  \item \textbf{Genericity}: Functions work on any data without type
    annotations or constraints.
  \item \textbf{Expressiveness}: Patterns like open recursion, duck typing,
    and runtime code generation are natural.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Type Error]\label{def:dynamic-type-error}
  A \emph{dynamic type error} is an error detected at runtime when an
  operation is applied to a value of an inappropriate type.  For example,
  adding a string to an integer, or calling a non-function.
\end{definition}

The cost of dynamic checking is that errors that could have been caught at
compile time are instead discovered during execution---possibly in
production, possibly after significant computation has already occurred.

\subsection{The Spectrum of Checking}\label{sec:checking-spectrum}

The static/dynamic dichotomy is too coarse.  Languages exist on a spectrum:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Fully static}: Haskell, Agda, Coq---all errors (of the
    checked kind) are caught at compile time.
  \item \textbf{Mostly static}: Rust, OCaml, Java---strong static checking
    with limited dynamic features (reflection, dynamic casts).
  \item \textbf{Gradual}: TypeScript, Python with type hints---static
    checking where annotations are provided, dynamic checking otherwise.
  \item \textbf{Mostly dynamic}: Ruby, JavaScript, Python (without hints)---
    dynamic checking with optional static analysis tools.
  \item \textbf{Fully dynamic}: Bash, early Lisp---nearly everything is
    determined at runtime.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
The choice between static and dynamic checking is not binary but a point on
a continuous spectrum.  Modern language design increasingly favors
\emph{gradual} approaches that allow programmers to choose the appropriate
level of checking for each part of their program.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Formal Comparison}\label{sec:formal-static-dynamic}

We can formalize the distinction using semantic frameworks.  In a statically
typed language, the typing judgment $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ is a
\emph{static} property: it can be checked without running the program.  In a
dynamically typed language, the analogous judgment is a \emph{dynamic}
property: it is checked as part of the evaluation relation.

\begin{semrules}{Dynamic Typing}
  \namedrule{Dyn-App}{
    e_1 \Downarrow \lambda x.\, e \quad
    e_2 \Downarrow v \quad
    e[v/x] \Downarrow v'
  }{
    e_1\; e_2 \Downarrow v'
  } \\[6pt]
  \namedrule{Dyn-App-Err}{
    e_1 \Downarrow c \quad c \neq \lambda x.\, e
  }{
    e_1\; e_2 \Downarrow \texttt{type\_error}
  }
\end{semrules}

In the statically typed counterpart, the \textsc{Dyn-App-Err} rule is
eliminated by the type system: the premise $\Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \to
\tau_2$ guarantees that $e_1$ evaluates to a function.

%% ============================================================
%% 13.4 Orthogonality and Minimalism
%% ============================================================
\section{Orthogonality and Minimalism}\label{sec:orthogonality-minimalism}

Two principles that frequently guide language design are
\emph{orthogonality} and \emph{minimalism}.  While related, they are
distinct.

\subsection{Orthogonality}\label{sec:orthogonality}

\begin{definition}[Orthogonality]\label{def:orthogonality}
  A language is \emph{orthogonal} if its features can be combined freely
  without unexpected interactions.  Formally, for any two features $F_1$ and
  $F_2$, the behavior of $F_1$ does not depend on whether $F_2$ is present,
  and vice versa.
\end{definition}

Orthogonality reduces the number of exceptional cases a programmer must
remember.  In an orthogonal language, the number of meaningful combinations
of features is the \emph{product} of the number of options for each feature,
rather than some smaller number constrained by interactions.

\begin{example}[Orthogonal: ML]\label{ex:orthogonal-ml}
  Standard ML is highly orthogonal.  Any expression can appear in any
  context: functions can return functions, records can contain functions,
  lists can contain records of functions, and so on.  The type system treats
  all types uniformly---there are no special cases for ``primitive'' vs.\
  ``user-defined'' types.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Non-Orthogonal: C]\label{ex:non-orthogonal-c}
  C is notably non-orthogonal.  Arrays cannot be returned from functions or
  assigned to variables.  Structs cannot contain themselves (only pointers to
  themselves).  The \texttt{const} qualifier interacts differently with
  pointers depending on position.  These restrictions create a large number
  of special cases.
\end{example}

\subsection{Minimalism}\label{sec:minimalism}

\begin{definition}[Minimalism]\label{def:minimalism}
  A language is \emph{minimal} if it provides the smallest set of features
  sufficient to express the desired class of computations.  A minimal
  language has no redundant features---every feature is necessary.
\end{definition}

Minimalism has several benefits:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Simplicity of specification}: Smaller languages are easier to
    define formally and implement.
  \item \textbf{Ease of learning}: Fewer features mean less to learn.
  \item \textbf{Composability}: A small set of orthogonal features can be
    combined in many ways.
  \item \textbf{Verifiability}: Smaller languages are easier to verify and
    prove properties about.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Minimal: Scheme]\label{ex:minimal-scheme}
  Scheme is famously minimal.  The entire language can be built from a small
  number of primitives: lambda, application, conditionals, quotes, and
  definitions.  Everything else---loops, records, modules, objects---is
  derived from these primitives.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Tension Between Orthogonality and Minimalism}\label{sec:orth-min-tension}

Orthogonality and minimalism can conflict.  A truly minimal language might
lack features that would make it more orthogonal.  Conversely, making a
language fully orthogonal might require adding features that compromise
minimalism.

\begin{example}[Tension: C arrays]\label{ex:tension-c-arrays}
  C's treatment of arrays illustrates this tension.  Making arrays
  first-class values (assignable, passable by value, returnable) would
  increase orthogonality but would also add complexity to the language and
  potentially reduce performance.  C chose minimalism (and performance) over
  orthogonality.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Core/Library Boundary}\label{sec:core-library}

A related principle is the proper placement of the boundary between the
\emph{core language} and the \emph{standard library}.  A well-designed
language has a small, elegant core and a rich library.

\begin{insight}
The ideal language has a kernel so small that its semantics can be written on
a single page, yet a library so rich that any practical program can be
written concisely.  The art lies in choosing the right kernel.
\end{insight}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Core Language Sizes}
  \label{tab:core-sizes}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Core Concepts} \\
    \hline
    Scheme & $\lambda$, \texttt{define}, \texttt{if}, \texttt{quote}, \texttt{set!} \\
    Lua & functions, tables, control structures \\
    C & types, functions, pointers, structs \\
    Haskell & types, classes, monads, laziness \\
    Rust & ownership, lifetimes, traits, pattern matching \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 13.5 Abstraction Mechanisms
%% ============================================================
\section{Abstraction Mechanisms}\label{sec:abstraction-mechanisms}

Abstraction is the fundamental tool for managing complexity.  A programming
language provides several mechanisms for abstraction, each addressing a
different aspect of program structure.

\subsection{Procedural Abstraction}\label{sec:procedural-abstraction}

The most basic form of abstraction is the \emph{procedure} (function,
subroutine, method).  Procedural abstraction allows a computation to be named,
parameterized, and reused.

\begin{definition}[Procedural Abstraction]\label{def:procedural-abstraction}
  \emph{Procedural abstraction} is the mechanism by which a computation is
  encapsulated in a named entity (a function or procedure) that can be
  invoked with different arguments.  The internal details of the computation
  are hidden from the caller.
\end{definition}

In the lambda calculus, procedural abstraction is captured by $\lambda$-abstraction:
\[
  \lambda x.\, e
\]
This binds $x$ in $e$, creating a function that can be applied to arguments.

\subsection{Data Abstraction}\label{sec:data-abstraction}

\emph{Data abstraction} separates the representation of data from its use.
The client of an abstract data type knows what operations are available but
not how they are implemented.

\begin{definition}[Data Abstraction]\label{def:data-abstraction}
  \emph{Data abstraction} is the separation of the interface of a data type
  (the operations that can be performed on it) from its implementation (the
  concrete representation and algorithms).
\end{definition}

There are two major approaches to data abstraction:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Abstract data types (ADTs)}: The representation is hidden,
    and operations are grouped with the type.  Examples: ML \texttt{struct}
    with signatures, Java classes with private fields.
  \item \textbf{Objects}: Data and methods are bundled together, and access
    is controlled by visibility modifiers.  Examples: Smalltalk, Java, C\#.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Type Abstraction}\label{sec:type-abstraction}

\emph{Type abstraction} allows programs to be written without specifying
concrete types.  This is the foundation of generic programming.

\begin{definition}[Type Abstraction]\label{def:type-abstraction}
  \emph{Type abstraction} is a binding construct for types, written
  $\Lambda \alpha.\, e$, that creates a function from types to terms.  It is
  the type-level analogue of $\lambda$-abstraction.
\end{definition}

In System~F (the polymorphic lambda calculus), type abstraction enables
functions like the polymorphic identity:
\[
  \Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda x{:}\alpha.\, x \;:\; \forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha
\]

\subsection{Module Abstraction}\label{sec:module-abstraction}

\emph{Module abstraction} provides a mechanism for organizing large programs
into separate, composable units with controlled interfaces.

\begin{definition}[Module]\label{def:module}
  A \emph{module} is a unit of program organization that bundles related
  declarations (types, values, functions) and controls their visibility
  through an interface (signature, export list).
\end{definition}

Module systems vary widely across languages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{ML modules}: First-class, parameterized by signatures,
    with functors for module-level functions.
  \item \textbf{Haskell modules}: Simpler, export-list based, with type
    classes for ad hoc polymorphism.
  \item \textbf{Rust modules}: File-based, with \texttt{pub} visibility,
    traits for interface abstraction.
  \item \textbf{Java packages}: Namespace organization with access modifiers.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Control Abstraction}\label{sec:control-abstraction}

\emph{Control abstraction} allows the programmer to define new control
structures.  This ranges from simple higher-order functions to full-blown
continuations and macros.

\begin{example}[Control Abstraction Levels]\label{ex:control-abstraction}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Higher-order functions}: \texttt{map}, \texttt{filter},
      \texttt{fold} abstract common iteration patterns.
    \item \textbf{Generators and coroutines}: \texttt{yield} abstracts
      stateful iteration.
    \item \textbf{Continuations}: First-class continuations allow arbitrary
      control flow to be captured and manipulated.
    \item \textbf{Macros}: Syntactic macros allow new syntax to be defined,
      effectively extending the language itself.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\subsection{Abstraction and Information Hiding}\label{sec:abstraction-hiding}

All forms of abstraction rely on \emph{information hiding}---the principle
that the internal details of an abstraction should be inaccessible to its
clients.  This is formalized by the notion of \emph{parametricity}:

\begin{theorem}[Reynolds' Parametricity]\label{thm:parametricity}
  Every polymorphic function of type $\forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha$ is
  the identity function (up to divergence).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
  A function $f : \forall \alpha.\, \alpha \to \alpha$ cannot inspect its
  argument (since it works for all types $\alpha$).  The only thing it can do
  is return the argument unchanged.  This is formalized using logical
  relations~\cite{reynolds1983types}.
\end{proof}

