%% 13.6 Safety Guarantees
%% ============================================================
\section{Safety Guarantees}\label{sec:safety-guarantees}

\emph{Safety} is the property that programs cannot exhibit certain classes of
undefined or erroneous behavior.  Different languages provide different levels
of safety, and the design of safety guarantees is one of the most important
aspects of language design.

\subsection{Memory Safety}\label{sec:memory-safety}

\begin{definition}[Memory Safety]\label{def:memory-safety}
  A language is \emph{memory safe} if every program in the language is
  guaranteed not to access memory that it does not own.  This precludes
  buffer overflows, use-after-free, double-free, and dangling pointer
  errors.
\end{definition}

Memory safety is critical for security.  According to Microsoft and Google,
approximately 70\% of serious security vulnerabilities in their software are
memory safety bugs~\cite{microsoft2019security, google2019security}.

Languages provide memory safety through different mechanisms:

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Memory Safety Mechanisms}
  \label{tab:memory-safety}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Languages} & \textbf{Cost} \\
    \hline
    Garbage collection & Java, Haskell, OCaml, Go & Runtime overhead \\
    Borrow checking & Rust & Compile-time complexity \\
    Region inference & Cyclone, ML Kit & Restricted patterns \\
    Manual (unsafe) & C, C++, Fortran & Security vulnerabilities \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Type Safety}\label{sec:type-safety}

\begin{definition}[Type Safety]\label{def:type-safety}
  A language is \emph{type safe} if every well-typed program is guaranteed
  not to perform operations that are inappropriate for the type of the
  data.  This precludes treating an integer as a pointer, calling a
  non-function, or accessing a field that does not exist.
\end{definition}

Type safety is typically ensured by a combination of static type checking
and runtime checks.  A language like Java is type safe (modulo
\texttt{unsafe} operations) because the JVM performs runtime type checks on
casts and array accesses.

\subsection{Thread Safety}\label{sec:thread-safety}

\begin{definition}[Thread Safety]\label{def:thread-safety}
  A language provides \emph{thread safety} guarantees if it prevents data
  races---concurrent accesses to shared memory where at least one access is
  a write and they are not properly synchronized.
\end{definition}

Rust's type system provides thread safety through the \texttt{Send} and
\texttt{Sync} traits, ensuring at compile time that data is not shared
unsafely across threads.

\begin{insight}
Safety guarantees are not free.  Every safety guarantee requires some
combination of programmer effort (annotations, discipline), compiler
complexity (analysis, checking), and runtime overhead (bounds checks, garbage
collection).  The art of language design is in finding the right balance.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Referential Transparency}\label{sec:referential-transparency}

\begin{definition}[Referential Transparency]\label{def:referential-transparency}
  An expression $e$ is \emph{referentially transparent} if it can be
  replaced with its value without changing the behavior of the program.
  A language is referentially transparent if all expressions are
  referentially transparent.
\end{definition}

Referential transparency is a powerful safety guarantee: it enables
equational reasoning, where the programmer can reason about programs by
substituting equals for equals.  Purely functional languages like Haskell
provide this guarantee; imperative languages generally do not.

\subsection{The Safety Hierarchy}\label{sec:safety-hierarchy}

We can organize safety guarantees in a hierarchy:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Memory safety}: No out-of-bounds or invalid memory access.
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: No type confusion.
  \item \textbf{Thread safety}: No data races.
  \item \textbf{Exception safety}: Invariants are preserved across exceptions.
  \item \textbf{Information flow security}: No unauthorized information leaks.
  \item \textbf{Resource safety}: Resources (files, locks) are properly released.
  \item \textbf{Termination}: Programs always terminate (total languages).
\end{enumerate}

Each level subsumes the ones below it and requires stronger guarantees from
the language.

%% ============================================================
%% 13.7 Case Studies
%% ============================================================
\section{Case Studies in Language Design}\label{sec:lang-design-case-studies}

We now examine how these principles manifest in five influential languages.

\subsection{C: The Portable Assembly}\label{sec:case-c}

\begin{historical}
C was designed by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs between 1969 and 1973, as the
implementation language for the Unix operating system~\cite{ritchie1974unix}.
Its design philosophy was ``trust the programmer'': provide powerful,
low-level features and let the programmer use them responsibly.
\end{historical}

C's design priorities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Efficiency}: C maps closely to machine hardware.  There is no
    garbage collector, no bounds checking, no runtime overhead for type
    checks.
  \item \textbf{Portability}: C programs can be compiled on many platforms
    with minimal changes.
  \item \textbf{Minimalism}: The core language is small.  The standard
    library is separate.
  \item \textbf{Low-level access}: Pointers, manual memory management, and
    bitwise operations give programmers direct hardware control.
\end{itemize}

C's design sacrifices:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Safety}: C is not memory safe, not type safe (casts can
    violate type invariants), and not thread safe.
  \item \textbf{Orthogonality}: Arrays are not first-class values; structs
    cannot contain themselves; function pointers have restrictions.
  \item \textbf{Abstraction}: Limited abstraction mechanisms compared to
    modern languages.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
C's success demonstrates that a language can be enormously influential
precisely \emph{because} of its minimalism and closeness to hardware, even
when it sacrifices safety and abstraction.  The lesson is that different
applications require different design trade-offs.
\end{insight}

\subsection{ML: The Functional Ideal}\label{sec:case-ml}

\begin{historical}
ML (Meta Language) was designed by Robin Milner at the University of
Edinburgh in the 1970s, originally as the tactic language for the LCF proof
assistant~\cite{milner1978theory}.  Its design emphasizes type safety,
abstraction, and mathematical elegance.
\end{historical}

ML's design priorities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: ML's type system guarantees that well-typed
    programs do not go wrong.  The Hindley--Milner type system provides
    complete type inference.
  \item \textbf{Abstraction}: ML provides powerful abstraction mechanisms:
    higher-order functions, pattern matching, parametric polymorphism, and
    the module system with functors.
  \item \textbf{Orthogonality}: ML is highly orthogonal.  Any expression can
    appear in any context.
  \item \textbf{Elegance}: The language has a clean mathematical foundation
    in the typed lambda calculus.
\end{itemize}

ML's type system is a landmark achievement:

\begin{typerules}{Hindley--Milner (Selected Rules)}
  \typeaxiom{\textsc{Var}}{
    \dfrac{}{\Gamma, x:\tau \vdash x : \tau}
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Abs}}{
    \Gamma, x:\tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash \lambda x.\, e : \tau_1 \to \tau_2
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{App}}{
    \Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_2 \to \tau \quad
    \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_2
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Gen}}{
    \Gamma \vdash e : \tau \quad
    \alpha \notin \mathrm{fv}(\Gamma)
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash e : \forall \alpha.\, \tau
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Inst}}{
    \Gamma \vdash e : \forall \alpha.\, \tau
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash e : \tau[\sigma/\alpha]
  }
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Java: The Industrial Workhorse}\label{sec:case-java}

\begin{historical}
Java was designed by James Gosling and colleagues at Sun Microsystems in the
early 1990s, initially for embedded systems and later for the
World Wide Web~\cite{gosling2000java}.  Its design emphasizes portability,
safety, and familiarity to C/C++ programmers.
\end{historical}

Java's design priorities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Safety}: Java is memory safe (garbage collected) and type
    safe (runtime type checks on casts and array accesses).
  \item \textbf{Portability}: The JVM provides a ``write once, run anywhere''
    platform.
  \item \textbf{Familiarity}: Java's syntax is deliberately similar to C/C++,
    lowering the barrier for existing programmers.
  \item \textbf{Object-orientation}: Everything is an object (except
    primitives).  Classes, inheritance, and interfaces are the primary
    structuring mechanisms.
\end{itemize}

Java's design sacrifices:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Minimalism}: Java has accumulated many features over time
    (generics, lambdas, records, sealed classes), making the language
    increasingly complex.
  \item \textbf{Orthogonality}: The distinction between primitives and
    objects, the special treatment of arrays, and the restrictions on
    generics (type erasure) all reduce orthogonality.
  \item \textbf{Expressiveness}: Java's verbose syntax and lack of features
    like higher-order functions (until Java~8) and pattern matching make
    some programs unnecessarily long.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rust: Safety Without Garbage Collection}\label{sec:case-rust}

\begin{historical}
Rust was designed by Graydon Hoare at Mozilla Research, first appearing in
2010~\cite{klabnik2019rust}.  Its central innovation is the ownership system,
which provides memory safety and thread safety without garbage collection.
\end{historical}

Rust's design priorities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory safety without GC}: Rust's ownership and borrowing
    system ensures memory safety at compile time, with no runtime garbage
    collector.
  \item \textbf{Zero-cost abstractions}: High-level features (iterators,
    closures, generics) compile to code as efficient as hand-written
    low-level code.
  \item \textbf{Thread safety}: The type system prevents data races through
    the \texttt{Send} and \texttt{Sync} traits.
  \item \textbf{Control}: Programmers can drop down to unsafe code when
    necessary, with explicit \texttt{unsafe} blocks.
\end{itemize}

Rust's ownership system is built on three rules:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Each value has exactly one \emph{owner}.
  \item When the owner goes out of scope, the value is \emph{dropped}.
  \item A value can have either one mutable reference or any number of
    immutable references, but not both simultaneously.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
Rust demonstrates that it is possible to achieve memory safety and thread
safety without garbage collection, by shifting complexity from runtime to
compile time.  The cost is a steep learning curve and a more complex type
checker.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Python: The Scripting Powerhouse}\label{sec:case-python}

\begin{historical}
Python was created by Guido van Rossum in the late 1980s and early 1990s,
emphasizing readability and programmer productivity~\cite{vanrossum1995python}.
Its design philosophy is captured in the ``Zen of Python'': ``Simple is
better than complex.  Readable code is better than obscure code.''
\end{historical}

Python's design priorities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Readability}: Python's indentation-based syntax enforces a
    clean visual structure.
  \item \textbf{Productivity}: Dynamic typing, rich standard library, and
    concise syntax enable rapid development.
  \item \textbf{Flexibility}: Python's dynamic nature allows powerful
    metaprogramming (decorators, metaclasses, monkey patching).
  \item \textbf{Batteries included}: A comprehensive standard library
    reduces the need for third-party packages.
\end{itemize}

Python's evolution toward gradual typing (with type hints, \texttt{mypy},
\texttt{Pyright}) illustrates the modern trend of adding static checking to
dynamically typed languages without breaking existing code.

