\section{Choosing Exhaustiveness for Evolvable Tagged Types}

A language that requires exhaustive matches over closed variants
makes omissions visible at compile time. The cost is that adding a
constructor may break clients, even when its introduction is harmless
for some of them. To see the policy in isolation, consider a match
over a finite list of distinct constructor tags. Each arm has either a
single tag or a wildcard; patterns ignore any payload, arms run in
order, and there are no guards. Tags have already been resolved to
constructors of the scrutinee type. The checker reports shadowed arms
and returns the tags still uncovered after the final arm.

\begin{sudocode}[Check ordered arms of a closed variant]
\Function{Coverage}{$T,\mathrm{arms}$}
  \State $U\gets$ a copy of $T$
  \ForAll{arms $a$ in source order}
    \If{$a$ is a wildcard}
      \State $H\gets$ a copy of $U$ \Comment{Only remaining tags}
    \Else
      \State $H\gets U\cap\{\mathrm{tag}(a)\}$
    \EndIf
    \If{$H=\varnothing$}
      \State Report shadowed arm $a$ \Comment{Earlier arms win}
    \EndIf
    \State $U\gets U\setminus H$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $U$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Start with tags $\{\text{Ready},\text{Busy}\}$ and arms
\texttt{Ready}, \texttt{Busy}. The first arm leaves only
\texttt{Busy}; the second clears the set. Add a constructor
\texttt{Paused}. The same arms remove the same two tags but now
leave \texttt{Paused}, making the missing decision explicit.
Replace the second arm with a wildcard: it consumes both
\texttt{Busy} and \texttt{Paused}. Append an explicit
\texttt{Paused} arm and the checker warns that it is shadowed.
The wildcard avoids the compatibility break but silently commits
the new constructor to the old fallback behavior.

\paragraph{Invariant and design limit.}
After each arm, $U$ contains exactly the tags that would reach the
next one. A guarded pattern would break the rule that a matching
tag is always removed; full pattern checking must account for the
payload and possible guard failure. For a public extensible variant,
another policy is to require a fallback at clients from the outset:
the current declaration cannot enumerate future tags. Choosing
between these policies controls where an evolving interface asks
users to make a decision, not just how the checker represents sets.

\section{Summary and Outlook}\label{sec:lang-design-summary}

Language design is an exercise in managing trade-offs.  No language can
maximize all desirable properties simultaneously.  The designer must
understand the application domain, the target users, and the available
technology, and then make informed choices about which properties to
prioritize.

The six principles we have examined---expressiveness, analyzability,
orthogonality, minimalism, abstraction, and safety---form a framework for
evaluating design decisions.  But they do not determine a unique answer.
Different languages make different trade-offs, and all have found their
niche.

The trend in modern language design is toward languages that provide
\emph{more} of everything: more safety (Rust), more expressiveness (Scala,
Kotlin), more analyzability (TypeScript, F\#).  This is made possible by
advances in type theory, program analysis, and compiler technology.  The
languages of the future will likely continue to push the boundaries of what
can be statically guaranteed while remaining practical and usable.

In the chapters that follow, we explore the specific mechanisms through which
these principles are realized: paradigms (\Cref{ch:paradigms}), grammars
(\Cref{ch:formal-grammars}), lexical structure (\Cref{ch:lexical-structure}),
names and binding (\Cref{ch:names-binding}), scoping (\Cref{ch:scoping}),
modules (\Cref{ch:modules}), and type systems (\Cref{ch:type-systems-overview}--\Cref{ch:gradual-typing}).
